          "basis_type": "NEGATIVE RESULTS - four hypotheses tested leave-one-JOB-out and REJECTED, no engine change made",
          "lesson": "QUANTITY. 'A supplier prices a run of one differently from a run of sixty' is true as commerce and is not in this money. Each line's own supply GBP/m2 over the median for its OWN code and band: qty 1 -> 1.035, 5-8 -> 0.957, 41+ -> 1.000. Log-log slope -0.023, R^2 0.0109 over 486 lines; per JOB median R^2 0.0034. A 3.5% effect against errors of 30-50%. THE TRAP: at qty 1 the MEAN ratio is 1.149 against a MEDIAN of 1.035, and the two worst documents in the archive are both single-unit one-line jobs, so the hypothesis fits the worst list perfectly and is still the wrong way round. DATE. Unanswerable here: all 29 documents are 2026, dated 25/03 to 29/07, a span of 0.2 years. It does settle for free that the learned rates need no inflation adjustment to be quoted today. MIN_LEARNED_N. Never tested, and already at its optimum: 3 -> 13.91, 5 -> 13.51, 6 -> 13.28, 8 -> 13.29, 10 -> 13.48. Lowering it is the worse half - a bucket of three lines really is anecdote and the register beats it. COARSER SIZE BANDS. The edges had been tested finer and sideways, never coarser, and the product code already encodes size. One bucket per code scores 19.38% against the current (2.0, 5.0) at 13.28%, so the band does work the code does not.",
