      "to": "adam",
      "subject": "Redditch Library - pricing document built at GBP 89,218.65, undercuts Joedan by 1.62%, and the strip-out line is an allowance you need to set",
      "body": "Adam,\n Redditch Library. The pricing document and the proposal are both built and attached. Nothing has\ngone to Pride.\n THE NUMBER\n TENDER SUM GBP 89,218.65, gross of 2.5% Main Contractor's Discount, excluding VAT.\n That undercuts Joedan by GBP 1,468.52, which is 1.62% on the number printed in the pack. Their\nGBP 90,687.17 is also gross of 2.5% MCD, so the two figures are now stated on exactly the same\nbasis and can be put side by side. Net to Pride, ours is GBP 86,988.18 against their GBP 88,419.99.\n MATCHING JOEDAN'S INCLUSIONS IS WHAT MOVED THE PRICE, AND IT IS NOT FREE\n You asked for the same inclusions and exclusions as Joedan. I have taken their lists verbatim from\npages 151 to 153 of the pack - 11 inclusions, 25 exclusions - with one deliberate difference, the\nwarranty, which I come to below.\n Four of their inclusions were not in the benchmark I sent you at 19:56, and three of them cost money:\n Removal of the existing windows and doors. Their clause 12 includes it. So this settles the\nstrip-out question by the back door - if we match their inclusions, strip-out is in our price.\n Perimeter sealing at the time of installation, their clause 15. GBP 1,571.45 at 314.29 linear\nmetres. Our house documents have always offered mastic as an OPTIONAL extra, and page 70 clause\n11 of this pack REQUIRES it. That is the Princess Beatrice mistake exactly - offering as an\noption work we are obliged to do, and inviting the QS to strike it. It is now inside the sum and\nI have removed the OPTIONAL block from the face of the document so nobody can strike it.\n 45mm cellular uPVC cloaking profile to the internal perimeter, their clause 13. NO RATE EXISTS\nfor this anywhere in our data. It is carried as included scope with the sealing line and priced\nat nothing. It needs to come back on a supplier quote - roughly 314 linear metres of trim.\n Trickle vents, their clause 4, which we already had.\n THE STRIP-OUT LINE IS AN ALLOWANCE AND YOU NEED TO LOOK AT IT\n GBP 3,000 net. GBP 69.77 per opening, 43 openings, metal frames, occupied library.\n I want to be plain that this is not a rate. There is no strip-out rate in Fenster's records - I\nsearched 362 archive workbooks for one last night and the promising hits turned out to be item\nreference numbers in unpriced schedules. GBP 3,000 is a figure I have put in so that the client's\nown blank on page 70 can be filled and so that we match Joedan's scope. It is the only number in\nthe document that is not evidence-backed, and it is yours to move.\n Here is the trade-off, because the undercut and the strip-out allowance come out of the same pot:\n strip-out allowance tender sum undercut on Joedan\nGBP 2,000 88,193.02 GBP 2,494.15 (2.75%) GBP 46.51 an opening\nGBP 3,000 (as built) 89,218.65 GBP 1,468.52 (1.62%) GBP 69.77 an opening\nGBP 4,000 90,244.29 GBP 442.88 (0.49%) GBP 93.02 an opening\nGBP 4,432 90,687.17 nil - we match them GBP 103.07 an opening\n Every pound we add to strip-out is a pound off the undercut. You have taken 43 metal windows out\nof a building and I have not, so the per-opening column is the one to read.\n WHAT THE PRICE RESTS ON, AND WHY THE RFQ MATTERS TONIGHT\n The frames are on the BSW Severn Trent curve from last night - their quote to us for Pride's own\njob, 15/06, R-squared 0.9934 - converted to a second-supplier position using the measured factors\nBSW +5.7% against Aplus -1.6%. That conversion is worth GBP 3,936.12 of frame buy, and it is the\nwhole reason we can undercut them at all. Priced on BSW's own rates instead, with the sealing line\nin and NO strip-out at all, we come out at GBP 90,178.77 gross - GBP 508 under Joedan with nothing\nwhatever left to strip 43 openings. In other words, on the supplier we actually have a quote from,\nwe cannot do this.\n So the undercut currently depends on a supplier we have not asked. The RFQ is written, it is\nprice-free, and it has been sitting with you since 21:00. If it goes to Aplus or 4Ali as well as\nBSW, we find out whether this number is real. If we issue first and BSW's rates are what comes\nback, we are about GBP 3,900 underwater on our own document.\n THE TEN YEAR WARRANTY\n Done, and in three places: the executive summary, the inclusions list, and the terms on the back\npage which already carry it. Joedan offer twelve months on the same building (their clause 21), so\nit is the one line where I have deliberately NOT matched them, and the proposal says so in as many\nwords - &quot;in place of the twelve month product warranty customary in this sector.&quot;\n Worth knowing what it costs us: no supplier will back ten years. Whoever we buy from will offer\nsomething nearer Joedan's twelve months, and the difference sits with Fenster.\n TWO THINGS ON THE BACK PAGE OF OUR OWN PROPOSAL THAT A QS WILL FIND\n These are the house terms, not a Redditch slip, so they are on every proposal we send.\n 1. Our terms say the quotation is valid 30 days. The Form of Tender holds the sum open for 10\n weeks and the preliminaries say not less than 3 months. A return whose own terms expire\n before the client's validity period is non-compliant on its face.\n\n 2. Our terms demand a 50% deposit before commencement. The head contract is a JCT Minor Works\n with 5% retention reducing to 2.5%. No main contractor pays a 50% deposit under JCT. Joedan\n dealt with the same point head on - they stated their first release of retention instead.\n\n I have not edited the house terms. Fixing them here would fix one tender and leave the template\nwrong for the next one, which is exactly what happened with the file properties on Gordon Court.\nTell me which way you want it and I will do it once, at source.\n TWO SEPARATE FILES, DELIBERATELY\n The working pricing document keeps the product codes and the frame-cost columns live. Those sit\noutside the print area - and a print area protects a print, not the file. Anyone who opens the\n.xlsx and scrolls right reads our buy, our margin and the supplier name. The checks caught it.\n So the CLIENT COPY is a separate file wi"
